\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces The certificate families. The Pomerance-triple verification is exactly $(\tfrac  52+o(1))\,n$ multiplications $\bmod  \ p$. ${}^{\dagger }$Under the format's radical-capped revision of August 2026 (Definition\nonbreakingspace \ref {def:short2}), for which $O(n^2\log n)$ is the unconditional worst case with no precomputation (Proposition\nonbreakingspace \ref {prop:short2verif}); the original format's worst case was $O(n^2(\log n)^2)$, attained by its $k=0$ stratum ($=$ the restricted one-shot, Remark\nonbreakingspace \ref {rem:tightshort}). ${}^{\ddagger }$Heuristic for all $p$; provable for almost all $p$. Primality is in ZPP via \cite  {AH92}, but with certificates built on genus-2 Jacobians, not certificates of this format. The AKS--Bernstein row is discussed in Remark\nonbreakingspace \ref {rem:aks}; the deterministic finding entry is the Lenstra--Pomerance decision bound, whose exponent $c_0$ is effectively computable but has never been made explicit. The Pratt entry is the cost of factoring $p-1$ (and recursively below, a geometrically decreasing cost) by the number field sieve. The ECPP row's size and verification describe the customary smooth-cofactor descent ($\Theta (\log n)$ bits shed per step); the strict $m = 2q$ chain of \cite  {GK86} measures $\Theta (n^2)$ and $O(n^3\log n)$ (\S  \ref {sec:ecppverif}).}}{3}{table.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:summary}{{1}{3}{The certificate families. The Pomerance-triple verification is exactly $(\tfrac 52+o(1))\,n$ multiplications $\bmod \ p$. ${}^{\dagger }$Under the format's radical-capped revision of August 2026 (Definition~\ref {def:short2}), for which $O(n^2\log n)$ is the unconditional worst case with no precomputation (Proposition~\ref {prop:short2verif}); the original format's worst case was $O(n^2(\log n)^2)$, attained by its $k=0$ stratum ($=$ the restricted one-shot, Remark~\ref {rem:tightshort}). ${}^{\ddagger }$Heuristic for all $p$; provable for almost all $p$. Primality is in ZPP via \cite {AH92}, but with certificates built on genus-2 Jacobians, not certificates of this format. The AKS--Bernstein row is discussed in Remark~\ref {rem:aks}; the deterministic finding entry is the Lenstra--Pomerance decision bound, whose exponent $c_0$ is effectively computable but has never been made explicit. The Pratt entry is the cost of factoring $p-1$ (and recursively below, a geometrically decreasing cost) by the number field sieve. The ECPP row's size and verification describe the customary smooth-cofactor descent ($\Theta (\log n)$ bits shed per step); the strict $m = 2q$ chain of \cite {GK86} measures $\Theta (n^2)$ and $O(n^3\log n)$ (\S \ref {sec:ecppverif})}{table.1}{}}
